   "path": "SCENE.md",
   "role": "instructions",
   "content": "# Grid Wave\n\nA wave crosses the frame (outward from the centre, or left, right, up or down) and lifts the picture into a grid of solid tiles with a gap between them, tinted and shaded as they rise, then sets them down carrying the next scene. Runs its WebGL shader over two built-in panels (a stat and a bar chart, then a render queue; text and colours from variables), not over the host's scenes: 60 frames of the first panel, a 36-frame transition, then the second panel. To use it between your own scenes, copy the shader into your composition and feed it your two frames as textures.\n\nCategory: transitions. Length: 5.2 s. WebGL: yes.\n\nUse when:\n- A product or interface demo needs a cut with craft: the frame is handled as a surface rather than blended away.\n- The moment can afford well over a second; the wave has to travel far enough to read as a wave.\n- The two scenes are both structured and graphic, so the grid reads as a deliberate device.\n\nAvoid when:\n- The cut needs to be quick or invisible: use zoom-blur.\n- You want the frame to come apart into matter rather than be handled as tiles: use particle-dissolve.\n\nVariables:\n- label (string): Label, default \"Deploys this week\"\n- value (string): Value, default \"2,481\"\n- bars (string): Bars, default \"32,51,43,68,57,86,64,100\"\n- accent (color): Highlight, default \"#a1eebd\"\n- title (string): Title, default \"Render queue\"\n- items (string): Rows, default \"intro-scene.mp4 | Done; feature-tour.mp4 | Rendering; pricing-outro.mp4 | Queued\"\n- background (color): Background, default \"#101828\"\n- tile_size (number, 30-260): Tile size, default 90\n- wave_width (number, 0.04-0.5): Wave width, default 0.16\n- lift (number, 0-4): Lift, default 2\n- gap (number, 0-0.45): Gap, default 0.12\n- tint (color): Tint, default \"#8fb4ff\"\n- direction (enum): Direction, default \"ripple\"\n- resolution (number, 0.25-1): Resolution, default 0.5\n\nSource: https://github.com/Remocn/remocn. Licence: MIT. The scene file keeps the upstream notice.\n"
  },
